<p>When you receive a large sum — a performance bonus, EPF withdrawal, maturity payout, or proceeds from an asset sale — the question is not whether to invest but how to deploy it without taking concentrated timing risk. A lumpsum investment puts the full principal to work on day one, maximising compounding time; but if markets fall 20% in the first year, that entire principal absorbs the loss. The alternative — parking the amount in a liquid fund and transferring a fixed monthly portion to equity via a Systematic Transfer Plan (STP) — earns a liquid fund return on the full corpus while spreading equity entry over 6–12 months. This calculator shows what the lumpsum corpus becomes over time; compare that against your SIP corpus at the <a href="/india/sip-calculator/">SIP Calculator</a> to see which deployment strategy fits your situation.</p>

<p>The tax dimension matters at exit, not just at entry. Long-term capital gains (held over 12 months) on equity mutual funds are taxed at 12.5% on gains above ₹1.25 lakh per financial year under Section 112A of the Income Tax Act, effective 23 July 2024 (Finance Act 2024). Short-term gains (under 12 months) attract 20% tax under Section 111A. For a lumpsum with a single entry date, the LTCG/STCG determination is clean: one holding period from purchase to redemption. A ₹5L lumpsum at 12% for 10 years grows to approximately ₹15.5L; the ₹10.5L gain above ₹1.25L faces 12.5% LTCG tax of about ₹1.16L — leaving approximately ₹14.3L net. Budget 2026 made no changes to these rates. Use the <a href="/india/income-tax-calculator/">Income Tax Calculator</a> to model your post-tax return before making deployment decisions.</p>
